/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A stroke is one travel of the piston between TDC and BDC, which is half a crankshaft revolution, or 180° of crank angle (°CA).
   - The four-stroke SI and CI engines differ only in what enters in the intake stroke and how burning starts.
   - The small two-stroke petrol engine has no valves.
   - Because exhaust and transfer ports are open together, some fresh mixture flows straight out of the exhaust (short-circuiting).
   - A two-stroke diesel scavenges with air only, so short-circuited air wastes no fuel; fuel is injected after the ports close.
   - In uniflow scavenging, air enters through ports all round the bottom of the liner and leaves through exhaust valves in the head, so it sweeps the cylinder in one direction.
   - At the same speed a two-stroke cylinder fires twice as often as a four-stroke cylinder: once per revolution against once per two revolutions.
   - More strokes are also possible.
*/
