/* book exam traps: A two-stroke cycle does have exactly two strokes, one up and one down. The point is that each stroke does two jobs: compression above and induction below on the way up, power and gas exchange on the way down. | A two-stroke fires once per revolution and a four-stroke once per two revolutions. “Twice as often” is per second at the same speed, not “twice per revolution”. | Do not double the two-stroke power blindly; port losses and scavenging losses keep the real gain well short of double (1.5 times in the second worked example). | Scavenging is not supercharging. Scavenging sweeps burnt gas out with fresh charge; supercharging raises the density of the charge. | Two-stroke diesels do not use crankcase scavenging; they use a blower, and the big ones use uniflow with exhaust valves. */
