/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Petrol is volatile, so it is mixed with air before it reaches the cylinder: in a carburettor, by an injector in the inlet port, or (in direct-injection engines) early in the intake stroke.
   - If the compression ratio is too high, the last part of the mixture (the end gas) is heated by compression and by the advancing flame until it ignites all at once before the flame reaches it.
   - Load is controlled by the throttle, that is by the quantity of mixture, whose strength stays near the chemically correct ratio.
   - There is no spark plug and, usually, no throttle.
   - Near the end of compression an injector sprays diesel at high pressure as fine droplets.
   - Load is controlled by the amount of fuel injected, with the air flow almost the same at every load.
   - The high compression ratio gives a CI engine its better efficiency, but also peak pressures far above those of an SI engine (modern diesels exceed 150 bar), so the block, head, rods and bearings are heavier, and the engine runs at lower spe
   - Their exhausts need different treatment.
*/
