/* book Worked example: Q: An SI engine has a compression ratio of 9 and a CI engine a ratio of 18. Both start compression with air at 1 bar and 300 K. Assuming ideal (isentropic) compression with γ = 1.4, find the temperature and pressure at the end of compression in each.
   1. For isentropic compression T₂ = T₁ rγ−1 and p₂ = p₁ rγ.
   2. SI: 90.4 = 2.408, so T₂ = 300 × 2.408 = 722 K (449 °C); 91.4 = 21.67, so p₂ = 21.7 bar.
   3. CI: 180.4 = 3.178, so T₂ = 300 × 3.178 = 953 K (680 °C); 181.4 = 57.2, so p₂ = 57.2 bar.
   4. Check: the CI air is 231 K hotter and at 2.6 times the pressure; both changes make diesel fuel ignite much sooner.
   Answer: SI: 722 K and 21.7 bar. CI: 953 K and 57.2 bar. Real compression loses heat to the walls, so actual values are somewhat lower. */
